{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-109500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$ \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following map:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{\\,A-2I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $1$ step.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue  $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3f1d0e2bdbe34af20a523a05b939c7056afc42d4ad57f57464c26a385e82c3f"} {"question" "q-109500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109508" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109508" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+4I:$\n\n$$\nA+4I=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+4I:$\n\n$$\n(A+4I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+4I:$\n\n$$\n(A+4I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109508" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3f1d0e2bdbe34af20a523a05b939c7056afc42d4ad57f57464c26a385e82c3f"} {"question" "q-109508" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109508" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109508" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109506" "