ectors $v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$ of $A$\n- $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =15$ of $A$\n- $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$ of $A$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }w,\n$$\n\nand $w\\ne 0,$  the vector $v$ is  **not** a eigenvector corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$  of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nx\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n \ni.e. $A-15I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+20I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3f1d0e2bdbe34af20a523a05b939c7056afc42d4ad57f57464c26a385e82c3f"} {"question" "q-109431" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109431" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109431" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-I$ and $A+4I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n- $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n- $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nu\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-I=A-1I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+4I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ \ncorresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3f1d0e2bdbe34af20a523a05b939c7056afc42d4ad57f57464c26a385e82c3f"} {"question" "q-109236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/state.json" "file_sha256" "7675e59e53c101e2923daf8849f9d8b01deededeb97813dfa1e9905db5ff20d0"} {"question" "q-109244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-6I$ and $A+7I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above